PEANUTS: one added check in ooz's Mermaid decoder, applied after ooz-port.patch (build.sh). Without it a Mermaid stream asked for one byte more or less
than its true size still "decoded" in 254 of 303 tries, because the final literals are copied without checking how many there are; with it all 303 are
refused and every valid stream decodes as before. Kraken has this check already. Licence: GPL-3.0-or-later, as kraken.cpp (COPYING).

--- a/kraken.cpp
+++ b/kraken.cpp
@@ -3939,6 +3939,11 @@
   if (src_cur != src_end)
     return false;
 
+  // A chunk decoded to its true size uses its literals and its 16-bit offsets exactly; with a wrong
+  // size the final literal copy ran past them (or stopped short) and the chunk still "decoded"
+  if (lz->lit_stream != lz->lit_stream_end || lz->off16_stream != lz->off16_stream_end)
+    return false;
+
   return true;
 }
 
